+`receiver.py` provides waveform generation, an aligned-symbol discriminator and
+an abbreviated stream receiver. The stream receiver searches every feasible
+sample start and integer frequency offset from -10 to +10 Hz, then chooses the
+start, shared offset and bit sequence with the greatest summed quadrature tone
+energy. It knows sample rate, symbol length and number of symbols, but receives
+neither the transmitted bits nor their start and offset. It is an offline,
+fixed-length search: it does not detect an unknown message length, search
+arbitrary offsets, estimate fractional-Hz offsets, use streaming buffers, or
+handle clock drift. Timing and frequency accuracy are distinct from bit recovery.
+`aligned_check` is a narrower diagnostic which knows the symbol boundaries and
+uses nominal frequencies.
 
-Run the existing checks with `python3 -m unittest -v`. A basic timing reference is
-available with `python3 receiver.py`. These are deliberately inexpensive and run
-on the local computer. The waveform is illustrative; it is not a production
-modem, and the program makes no claims about hardware capacity.
+Synthetic scenarios live in `cases.json`, maintained by hand. The bit count and
+sample rate describe the observation; the generator applies the stated noise,
+delay and frequency offset. Every quick stream test and benchmark reads the
+current list. Tests require exact bit recovery on each listed case, reporting
+failed cases separately; adding a more difficult case can legitimately expose a
+receiver limitation. A seeded case is reproducible but does not establish a
+statistical error rate. The integer offset grid, fixed tone set and known message
+geometry are assumptions, not general receiver guarantees.
+
+Run `python3 -m unittest -v` for the quick checks and `python3 benchmark.py` (or
+`python3 receiver.py`) for all-case measurements. `python3 benchmark.py --seconds 2`
+increases the minimum measured wall duration per case. The benchmark first
+synthesizes data and prepares reusable oscillator tables, warms up, then repeats
+the full stream search on the same samples. Generation and preparation are not
+included in the processing rate; processing includes correlating the input,
+acquisition, timing/frequency grid search and decisions. Input duration includes
+leading and trailing samples. Wall seconds per input second gives the reciprocal
+real-time margin; CPU seconds per input second gives a single-core throughput
+estimate. The machine-wide ceiling multiplies that by the process affinity and,
+on cgroup v2, CPU quota upper bound. It assumes perfect parallel scaling of
+independent streams and is not a measured concurrent capacity. Scheduling,
+contention and preparation amortization can change actual margins.
+
+The benchmark also reports Python allocations traced for reusable tables and
+peak incremental processing workspace. This excludes the samples, interpreter,
+allocator overhead, and other memory. Approximate shared available memory comes
+from Linux `MemAvailable` and, when present, cgroup v2 remaining memory; it is
+not reserved. The allocation-only workspace count and percentage at the CPU
+ceiling assume separate tables per stream, ignore excluded memory and sharing,
+and are rough comparisons rather than safe capacity limits. These estimates and
+the single deterministic noise realization should not be used as production
+capacity or reliability claims.
